A||||8||Babygirl||1984 Engine Fires||||5||
Z||000000||Babygirl||09-11-2000||01:17 AM||||I read in a magazine, that the Fiero's in 1984 were notorious for engine fires.  I have a 1984 black SE and I was wondering what caused these fires.  The magazine article didn't elaborate on the issue.  Does anyone know?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||FIEROCIOUS||09-11-2000||02:05 AM||||Pore maintenance! <P>The owners of the 84 fires did not change their oil at the recomended interval. This in effect caused the engine with "cheap" rods to break and puncture a hole through the engine splashing hot engine oil on flamable Fiero insulation to catch fire and burn.<P>All 84 Fieros went through a masive recall (your car included) which A: fixed the Fiero engine fire problems and B: gave Fiero's a bad name! <P>Why cheap rods and flamable materials in the engine compartment? <BR>Pontiac did not want to invest too much money into the project, so they used substandard parts that did not meet the quality standard of other cars maketed under the GM name.<P>That's kinda short just of it anyway! Lot more politics involved but you need not be concerned with your Fiero!<P>Welcome to the forum!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||09-11-2000||08:39 AM||||I thought I read somewhere that about 600 1984 engine blocks were defective, developed cracks.  This allowed coolant to splash on the exhaust manifold causing the fires.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||09-11-2000||09:54 AM||||There are 3 reasons why the early 2.5L were more prone to engine fires. First was the connecting rods. (as stated in this and the other thread) Second was the coolant housing. The thin weld line cracked and spilled coolant on the hot exhaust. This was part of the recall where they changed out the housing. The third was that GM did not use a gasket for the valve cover (rocker arm cover) or the side lifter cover. (they just used a bead of silicone sealant) Over time (and poor maintance) oil leaked and eventually caught fire. On the last one about the gaskets, It was more prone on the Pontiac 6000/chevy celebrity due to the position of the exhaust.<BR>There is a Recall for the larger cars to remove the covers and put on gaskets but not for the Fiero. You can buy the gaskets at any auto parts stores.<P>Ford and Jeep both had problems with using the silicone sealant instead of gaskets on 4 cylinder engines during the 80's too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||jmw||09-11-2000||03:11 PM||||Well, I was told to call 1-800-PMCARES (that's Pontiac), give them my VIN and they could tell me if the work was done. Actually, that is not true. They don't keep any records like that, nor do dealers.  All they can tell you is which recalls were issued. You then have to take your Fiero to a Pontiac dealer, and they have to inspect it. I was told they may charge a diagnostic fee because they might have to break things down to determine if the work was done. If you're a good negotiator, I would think you might could get them to do it for free [they SHOULD, in my personal opinion].  I have not actually done this, since I've owned my Fiero since new and had it serviced by the dealer for the first 5 years, so have always  presumed the work was done. Also, there is a gold 2" x 3" sticker on my inside trunklid that refers to the engine fires, so I do know that was done. The other stuff I frankly am not sure of. <P>Anyway, according to Pontiac [PMCARES], there were 4 recalls issued on the '84:<P>#88-C-23A -- "maintenance"<BR>#86-C-13 -- "engine splash shield"<BR>#86-C-16 -- "rear disk brakes"<BR>#84-C-15B -- "engine rod failure" (fires)<P>If you take yours to the dealer, please let me know how it goes. I really need to take mine anyway, just to be on the safe side.<P>Good luck!!!  <P>[P.S. '84s may have a bad name, but I LOVE mine -- now more than ever.]<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000005||rockcrawl||09-11-2000||05:40 PM||||Just a warning to anyone who thinks thier Fiero is not going to burn because the recalls have been done. I recently bought an '86 SE 4cyl from a salvage yard. It had suffered an engine fire. While going through the car I found the plaque that was part of the recall (GM # 10167524). It was supposed to be installed on the uderside of the deck lid, but it was just put in the glove box with the owners manual. The fact that it wasn't installed doesn't matter, but it tells me the recall was done, and yet the car still burned. I can't determine the source of the fire. It melted the back window and burned out the interior as well. I carry a small fire extingusher in all of my Fieros just to be safe. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||4
Z||000006||rockcrawl||09-11-2000||05:46 PM||||Just in case anyone cares, the plaque reads...."Regular maintenance and proper service are very important for your Fiero. There have been engine fires in some Fieros - fires that have destroyed the cars - because they didn't get proper maintenance and service. Don't let this happen to your Fiero! Please read and follow the special full-color Fiero maintenance booklet".<P> I thought it was ironic to find this along with the maintenance booklet in a burned Fiero!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000007||rprevo||09-11-2000||08:20 PM|||| The car I put a V8 in was a 84 fire bomb.<BR>But the main cause of fire was one of the<BR>big red wires in the harness! Plus the<BR>oil soaked insulation caused some bummer<BR>conditions.<P>     Ray<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-11-2000||10:09 PM||||(This is from a comming cave article....)<P><BR>Some of the recall text is available on <A HREF="http://www.nhtsa.gov/" TARGET=_blank>The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA)</A> web site.<P>The full text is available thru <A HREF="http://www.alldata.com/" TARGET=_blank>ALLDATA</A> and the GM dealler network. It May be available thru the Popular Mechanic's service CD available at Wall/K mart(the PM disk is an ALLDATA product)<P>Engine fires were caused by a number of things. Oil getting on the insulation isn't one of them. Oil getting on the exhaust, specifically the catalyst, was and is the big one.<P>Some 84's had weak piston rods. <P>All 2.5l 4 cylinders got weak head bolts. (All applications, not just Fiero.) The car will usually run with one broken bolt but it will leak oil and/or coolant both of which are fire hazzards. The head bolts can be fixed w/o removing the head as long as you do one bolt at a time. (GM's instructions not mine. I'd just replace them all and the head gasket if it's leaked.)<P>Some of the 84's and maybe others were built minus a valve cover gasket. The seal would fail and leak.<P>Part of the blame was put on owners not changing oil etc. This was likely valid. One note I have is that USDOT estimates the average oil change interval is now over 5000 miles. For allot of cars it's likely around 10-15000<P>Are you in danger if recall work was not done? I can't really say. It depends allot on just what condition the car you have is in. Obviously any car that leaks oil or coolant is a fire hazzard. Unprotected wires near hot objects are also a good ignition source. This is true of every car on the road. <P>Part of what makes Fiero dificult is the placement of things in the engine compartment. In most FWD setups with these engines the catalyst and muffler are farther from the motor.||||reg||1
